  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nStarting to see the beginning portions of our heavy rain event \ncoming together early this afternoon. Showers and embedded \nthunderstorms ongoing but will just expand in coverage after around \n6PM tonight. Tonight we'll start pushing in a lot of tropical \nmoisture. For reference, PW values look to increase upwards of 1.6\" \nby late tonight. This would be a record breaking moisture surge as \nthe 90th percentile this time of year should be around 1\" with a \nmaximum reading of 1.3\". Thankfully storms will be moving at a good \nclip with storm motions at 20-30 mph but with heavy rainfall that \noccurred not even a week ago will only add to the flood threat. \nStorms will be moving but extreme moisture content in the air will \nallow storms to be efficient rain makers with the potential for \nstorms to run over an area multiple times leading to flash flooding. \n\nAll locations are expected to see rain during the overnight hours \ntonight and rain will continue through much of the day Tuesday \nbefore tapering off after sunset tomorrow. In response to the \napproaching tropical remnants coupled with the upper low, winds will \nbecome noticeably breezy. Sustained winds of 20-35 mph expected with \nstronger wind up to 60 mph. Heavy rainfall coupled with breezy winds \nmay knock over trees. Bulk shear values will be plentiful at 40-50kt \nTuesday afternoon which may give some organization to thunderstorms.\nHeavy rainfall leading to flash flooding will be the main impact but \neven a tornado or two will be possible. Hurricanes are notorious for \nhelping give spin in the atmosphere and models are suggesting some \ncurved hodographs and helicity. SPC has added a 2 percent tornado \nrisk encompassing Hudspeth, El Paso, and Otero counties. If a \ntornado does occur, it will be weak and short lived. \n\nShowers largely move out of the area late Tuesday and early \nWednesday morning. The center of the upper low will cross west to \neast over central NM through the day Wednesday. Low end rain showers \npossible in the Gila region and Sacramento Mtns with even more \nisolated showers elsewhere through the day Wednesday. The upper low \nslowly tries to lift out of the area but lingering energy looks to \nstay behind. A backdoor cold front looks to try and push in late \nThursday and early Friday but will only shave off a few degrees from \nThursdays highs compared to Friday. Some breezier east winds \npossible with the front. Much of the weekend looks mostly dry but \nmore rain chances possible early next week?",
